WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.280
Where does a tree's mass come from?

2
00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:09.920
A willow in a pot, weighed long ago, turns that into a puzzle.

3
00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:13.280
Here is what the man who weighed it reported.

4
00:00:14.383 --> 00:00:19.663
In the sixteen hundreds, a man planted a five-pound willow stem in two hundred

5
00:00:19.663 --> 00:00:20.943
pounds of dried soil.

6
00:00:22.183 --> 00:00:26.543
Five years later, he reported a tree of about one hundred sixty-nine pounds.

7
00:00:27.923 --> 00:00:30.083
The soil was only about two ounces lighter.

8
00:00:31.183 --> 00:00:33.963
So soil loss could not account for the tree's gain.

9
00:00:35.183 --> 00:00:38.083
He weighed it alive, so part of its gain was water.

10
00:00:39.303 --> 00:00:42.423
But dry the tree, and plenty of solid matter is left.

11
00:00:43.467 --> 00:00:45.387
Make a guess before we zoom in.

12
00:00:46.467 --> 00:00:50.067
If you dried this tree, how much of what is left came from the soil?

13
00:00:51.427 --> 00:00:53.647
Most of it, about half, or almost none?

14
00:01:00.133 --> 00:01:06.153
Almost none. Soil gives a plant water and a small share of minerals, such as

15
00:01:06.153 --> 00:01:11.253
nitrogen. Most of a plant's dry mass comes from something you cannot see.

16
00:01:12.413 --> 00:01:13.513
It comes from the air.

17
00:01:14.517 --> 00:01:20.897
Zoom into a leaf. Carbon dioxide from the air drifts in through tiny pores called

18
00:01:20.897 --> 00:01:24.177
stomata. Water climbs up from the roots.

19
00:01:25.557 --> 00:01:28.617
Inside a leaf cell, a green chloroplast catches light.

20
00:01:29.667 --> 00:01:34.007
Its green pigment, chlorophyll, absorbs mostly red and blue light.

21
00:01:35.187 --> 00:01:37.347
That captured light powers photosynthesis.

22
00:01:38.827 --> 00:01:42.847
Water is split, and carbon from carbon dioxide is built into sugar.

23
00:01:43.967 --> 00:01:49.027
The atoms are rearranged into one glucose, a sugar with six carbon atoms.

24
00:01:50.387 --> 00:01:53.767
Six molecules of oxygen gas are released through the stomata.

25
00:01:55.400 --> 00:02:01.800
Now count. Going in, there are six carbon, twelve hydrogen and eighteen oxygen

26
00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:05.520
atoms. Coming out, there are exactly the same.

27
00:02:06.740 --> 00:02:09.100
No atom is made, and none is destroyed.

28
00:02:10.420 --> 00:02:12.060
This is conservation of matter.

29
00:02:13.150 --> 00:02:14.610
So what did the light add?

30
00:02:15.830 --> 00:02:16.930
Not a single atom.

31
00:02:18.410 --> 00:02:19.410
Light adds energy.

32
00:02:20.670 --> 00:02:25.370
Part of that energy is stored as chemical energy in the new arrangement of atoms.

33
00:02:26.500 --> 00:02:32.660
Glucose and oxygen together hold more chemical energy than the carbon dioxide and

34
00:02:32.660 --> 00:02:33.720
water they came from.

35
00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:37.500
Think of a stretched spring, held ready to let go.

36
00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:39.780
Back to the willow.

37
00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:45.500
The plant links glucose into long chains of cellulose, the tough fiber of cell

38
00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:48.660
walls. Wood is mostly cell walls.

39
00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:54.180
So most of the trunk's dry mass was built from carbon dioxide and water.

40
00:02:55.283 --> 00:02:56.843
Look at the dried tree again.

41
00:02:58.183 --> 00:03:01.543
Most of its dry mass came from carbon dioxide in the air.

42
00:03:02.863 --> 00:03:04.123
Water supplied the hydrogen.

43
00:03:05.443 --> 00:03:08.063
Only a small, essential share came from the soil.
